ing Implications and Equivalences" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198267 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198113 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198267 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199057 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199058 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199059 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199060 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199061 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199062 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199063 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Statements With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199057 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048580 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198522 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b9abbff1-085d-5330-8eee-8472c1138825" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407631 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171780" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $x$ is divisible by $6,$ then $x$ is even.\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62cb5939-3daf-5feb-b091-399708f8a47f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186198716 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is even.\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x$ is not divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not divisible by $6,$ or $x$ is even." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199058 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1061 144906 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49a55e7b-da8f-5b9b-aca0-ea5672a75f90" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199531 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199058 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199059 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199058 #edb/ref 1061 144907 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199058 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5bf2cacb-1ce2-5c76-9cc6-0e6f2a29dc49" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199058 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198265 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199059 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199060 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199059 #edb/ref 1061 144910 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199059 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62147a48-4a50-55e4-a548-b950bc9af5a9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199059 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199060 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199061 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199060 #edb/ref 1061 144911 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199060 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f2be205-1493-56f5-8512-a42f382c50fa" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199060 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199609 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199062 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199061 #edb/ref 1061 144912 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e2b4e61-5f88-5898-bcf9-9d4fae86c41e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198266 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199062 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199063 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199062 #edb/ref 1061 144915 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b35ba584-56c8-537f-b6d7-15de6671592a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199532 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199063 #edb/ref 1061 144916 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199063 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc5d356c-d8d2-517d-839c-18b823820728" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199063 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198267 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199531 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$P\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\ & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$U = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is $$\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{2,3,5,7}}={1,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $$T_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={1,4,6,8,9}\\cup {1,3,9} \\\\ & ={1,3,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199531 #edb/ref 1065 7992 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199531 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a295ff-82fc-5afe-8aa1-b24421f2c474" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199531 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199532 #edb/ref 1064 "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}P\\Rightarrow Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P\\Rightarrow Q)∧(Q\\Rightarrow P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199532 #edb/ref 1065 7993 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199532 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "599dc11e-cbd2-596c-bb72-4f73b83034ab" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199532 #edb/ref 1067 "Logical Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199533 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true). $$x + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3$$ This statement is true because $$\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).$$\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false). $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2$$ This statement is false because $$\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).$$ However, the following statement is true: $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199533 #edb/ref 1065 7995 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199533 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e9f21f0-8dbb-5442-968e-b1fb4425fef7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199533 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalence of Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199609 #edb/ref 1064 "For predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ the equivalence $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nIt follows that the truth set of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ can be found using\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's find the truth set of $P(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $1-5x < 11$\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq -1$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the corresponding inequality: $$\\begin{aligned}1-5x & <11 \\\\ -5x & <10 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}$$ So, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = (-2, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_P = (-\\infty,-2].$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $T_Q = [-1, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_Q = (-\\infty,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-2,\\infty )\\cap [-1,\\infty ) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty ) \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cap (-\\infty ,-1) \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\ & =[-1,\\infty )\\cup (-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [-1,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199609 #edb/ref 1065 9146 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199609 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "196ed409-81bb-5e86-bf1c-5e34aa4fb041" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186199609 #edb/ref 1067 "Truth Sets of Equivalences" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407639 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407640 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407641 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407642 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407643 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Statements With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186407640 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046679 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048580 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407604 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5c348c4b-2fa7-526a-b320-4fc1a8473bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407605 #edb/ref 1081 "images/3e/3e